window.docContents[19].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations":{"contents":"Translation by a fixed vector t is an automorphism of the Hamming cube: it\npreserves the Hamming distance since (t + x) - (t + y) = x - y. Under the\nisomorphism hypercubeIsoHamming, this corresponds to the automorphism\nhypercube.translate of Q[n]. There we had to define xor on HypercubeVert n\nand prove its basic properties (such as HypercubeVert.xor_xor) by hand. On\nFin n → Bool, addition is pointwise xor, and the group structure (associativity,\n0, negation) comes for free from Mathlib, as do the characteristic-two facts once\nwe have the BooleanRing instance above.\n\nTranslation by t (pointwise xor) as an automorphism of the Hamming cube./-- Translation by t (pointwise xor) as an automorphism of the Hamming cube. -/\ndef hypercubeHamming.translate {n : ℕ} (t : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := Equiv.addLeft t\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    intro x y\n    grind [Equiv.coe_addLeft, hypercubeHamming_adj, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.translate_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hypercubeHamming.translate t x = t + x := rfl\n\n\nPermuting the coordinates by σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) also preserves the Hamming\ndistance, and hence is an automorphism of the Hamming cube.\n\nlemma _root_.hammingDist_comp_perm {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] {β : Type*}\n    [DecidableEq β] (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (x y : ι → β) :\n    hammingDist (x ∘ σ) (y ∘ σ) = hammingDist x y := Finset.card_equiv σ (by simp)\n\n\nPermutation of coordinates by σ as an automorphism of the Hamming cube;\nx is sent to x ∘ σ.symm so that the i-th coordinate of the image is the\nσ⁻¹ i-th coordinate of x./-- Permutation of coordinates by σ as an automorphism of the Hamming cube;\nx is sent to x ∘ σ.symm so that the i-th coordinate of the image is the\nσ⁻¹ i-th coordinate of x. -/\ndef hypercubeHamming.permute {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := σ.arrowCongr (Equiv.refl Bool)\n  map_rel_iff' {x y} := by\n    rw [hypercubeHamming_adj, hypercubeHamming_adj,\n      ← hammingDist_comp_perm σ.symm x y]\n    rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.permute_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n))\n    (x : Fin n → Bool) : hypercubeHamming.permute σ x = x ∘ σ.symm := rfl\n\n\nIn the automorphism group the inverse f⁻¹ is by definition f.symm, so the\nformulae for the inverses of translations and coordinate permutations hold by rfl.\nSince -t = t, the inverse of translation by t is translation by t itself, so\ntranslations are involutions (characteristic two again).\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.translate_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming.translate t)⁻¹ x = t + x := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.permute_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n))\n    (x : Fin n → Bool) : (hypercubeHamming.permute σ)⁻¹ x = x ∘ σ := rfl\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.1. Translations and Coordinate Permutations","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-grind":{"contents":"grind is the other automated tactic we use throughout this guide, and it works\nquite differently from simp. Rather than rewriting the goal, it assumes that the\ngoal is false and looks for a contradiction. Along the way it collects facts, keeps\ntrack of which terms are equal, splits into cases, applies lemmas whenever it meets\nterms that match them, and uses built-in procedures for linear arithmetic over ℕ\nand ℤ and for algebra in commutative rings. It only ever finishes a goal: if it\ncannot, it fails and lists the facts it had collected, which often shows what it was\nmissing.\n\ngrind does not use the simp set. Unlike simp, it always uses all the hypotheses\nin the local context (so passing one, as in grind [h], is an error). Apart from\nthese and its built-in reasoning, it only uses the lemmas tagged with a grind\nattribute and those we pass to it, as in grind [foo, bar]. Very few of Mathlib's\nlemmas about graphs are tagged for grind, which is why our grind calls often list\nlemmas.\n\nLike simp, grind unfolds an abbrev but not a def.\n\nexample (x : ℕ) : pathNat.Adj x (x + 1) := by grind\n\n\nexample (x : ℕ) : pathNatFromRel.Adj x (x + 1) := by grind [pathNatFromRel_adj]\n\n\nexample (x : ℕ) : pathNatFromEdgeSet.Adj x (x + 1) := by\n  grind [pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj]\n\n\nPlain grind fails on the last two goals: it cannot see inside the def\npathNatFromRel. It does unfold the abbrev pathNatFromEdgeSet, but Mathlib's\nfromEdgeSet_adj is not a grind lemma, so it is left with a set of edges in\nSym2 ℕ that it cannot work with. For the last goal grind [fromEdgeSet_adj] would\nalso work, but the tidied-up statement of pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj (the lemma that\nsimp never uses) is more useful in general. With it, grind proves that pathNat\nand pathNatFromEdgeSet are equal without rewriting first, whereas\ngrind [fromEdgeSet_adj] fails here.\n\nexample : pathNat = pathNatFromEdgeSet := by\n  ext a b\n  grind [pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj]\n\n\nLike simp?, grind? suggests a call grind only [...] that uses just the lemmas\nthat were needed (it may also offer other forms of the call).\n\nexample (x : ℕ) : pathNatFromRel.Adj x (x + 1) := by\n  grind? [pathNatFromRel_adj] -- Try these: grind only [pathNatFromRel_adj], ...\n\n\nInstead of passing a lemma to each grind call, we can tag it with a grind\nattribute, so that every later grind call can use it. Most such tags in Mathlib are\n@[grind =], on equations or ↔s characterizing a definition (like\npathNatFromRel_adj): grind then uses the lemma whenever it meets a term matching\nits left-hand side. (Similarly @[grind →] uses an implication whenever it meets\nterms matching its hypotheses.) Here we add the attribute for a single example only,\nwith attribute [local grind =] … in.\n\nattribute [local grind =] pathNatFromRel_adj in\nexample (x : ℕ) : pathNatFromRel.Adj x (x + 1) := by grind\n\n\nTags should be added sparingly, since they affect every later grind call, and a\nlemma that matches too many terms can make grind slow. The command\n#grind_lint inspect foo warns if the lemma foo leads grind into long chains of\nlemma applications. In this guide we do not tag any lemmas for grind. Instead each\ncall lists the lemmas it needs, which also shows the reader which facts the proof\nuses.\n\nFinally, grind is not designed for problems that need a huge number of case splits,\nsuch as checking by brute force that a particular graph has no 3-colouring. For\nfinite checks like this we use decide, introduced in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph\u0009Constructing a SimpleGraph","header":"2.7.3. Grind","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-grind"},"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nInformally, a subgraph of a graph G is a graph H with V(H) ⊆ V(G) and\nE(H) ⊆ E(G). We have already seen the subgraph order H ≤ G on SimpleGraph V,\nwhich describes subgraphs with the same vertex set (or more precisely the same\nvertex type). For a general subgraph we need to allow the vertex set to vary. Mathlib\nformalizes this with the type SimpleGraph.Subgraph.\n\nIn this chapter we introduce SimpleGraph.Subgraph and the bounded lattice structure\nof subgraphs. We then look at ways of constructing subgraphs: from vertices, edges\nand walks, by taking induced subgraphs, and by deleting vertices and edges. Since a\nSubgraph is not a SimpleGraph we next consider the ways to convert between the\ntwo (Subgraph.coe, Subgraph.spanningCoe and toSubgraph), spanning subgraphs,\nand the parts of the SimpleGraph API that have to be duplicated for Subgraph.\nSince we now have four different ways of talking about subgraphs, we compare them and\ndiscuss when to use each. As an application we use the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem\n(colorable_of_forall_finite_subgraph_colorable) to extend our greedy colouring\nbound to infinite graphs (colorable_of_degree_le). We then introduce maps and\ncomaps of subgraphs, with subgraphs of the Mycielskian as a worked example.\n\nMatchings are subgraphs, so we next define the matching number ν G of a graph and\nprove weak duality ν G ≤ τ G (matchingNum_le_vertexCoverNum), where τ G is the\nvertex cover number. We then compute the matching number of the hypercube\n(matchingNum_hypercube_succ) and bound that of the Mycielskian\n(two_mul_matchingNum_le_matchingNum_mycielskian).\n\nThe final section is more advanced: we introduce partial colourings (ColoringOn),\ncolourings that need only be valid on a given set of vertices. These are the main\ntool in our proof of Brooks' theorem in the next chapter\nBrooks' Theorem.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"9. Subgraphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs"},"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps":{"contents":"Subgraph.map is a pushforward operation: given a graph homomorphism f : G →g K,\nbetween G : SimpleGraph V and K : SimpleGraph W, and a subgraph H : G.Subgraph,\nH.map f is the subgraph of K whose vertex set is f '' H.verts and whose edges\nare the images f u — f v of edges u — v of H. Note the contrast with\nSimpleGraph.map (Maps), which takes a plain function: here f\nmust be a homomorphism, since the image has to be a subgraph of the given graph K.\n\n#source Subgraph.map\n\n\nThere is also Subgraph.comap which is a pullback operation: given a homomorphism\nf : G →g K and a subgraph H of K, H.comap f is the subgraph of G whose\nvertex set is the preimage f⁻¹' H.verts and whose edges are those edges u — v of\nG for which f u and f v are adjacent in H.\n\n#source Subgraph.comap\n\n\nAs for simple graphs (SimpleGraph.map_le_iff_le_comap), map f and comap f form\na Galois connection. Here this holds for every homomorphism f, not just for\nembeddings: a homomorphism never sends two adjacent vertices to the same vertex, so\nno edge is lost when we map forwards.\n\n#check Subgraph.map_le_iff_le_comap -- H.map f ≤ H' ↔ H ≤ H'.comap f\n\n\nWe have Subgraph.inclusion which gives the graph homomorphism between two subgraphs\nH ≤ K (coerced to SimpleGraph on their respective vertex sets).\n\n#check Subgraph.inclusion -- {H K : G.Subgraph} (h : H ≤ K) : H.coe →g K.coe\n\n\nMore generally, a homomorphism f : G →g K restricts to a homomorphism between\nsubgraphs A of G and B of K whenever B contains the image A.map f.\nMathlib only has the special case Subgraph.inclusion (where f = Hom.id), so we\ndefine it ourselves since we will need it later.\n\nA homomorphism f : G →g K restricts to a homomorphism from a subgraph A of\nG to any subgraph B of K containing A.map f./-- A homomorphism f : G →g K restricts to a homomorphism from a subgraph A of\nG to any subgraph B of K containing A.map f. -/\ndef Subgraph.homOfMapLE {W : Type*} {K : SimpleGraph W}\n    (f : G →g K) {A : G.Subgraph} {B : K.Subgraph} (h : A.map f ≤ B) :\n    A.coe →g B.coe where\n  toFun v := ⟨f v, h.1 ⟨v, v.2, rfl⟩⟩\n  map_rel' hvw := h.2 ⟨_, _, hvw, rfl, rfl⟩\n\n\nWe also have Subgraph.hom which gives the graph homomorphism from a subgraph H to\nthe original graph G.\n\n#check Subgraph.hom -- .. (H : G.Subgraph) : H.coe →g G\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.map with Subgraph.hom gives Subgraph.coeSubgraph which\nallows us to take a subgraph of a subgraph coerced to SimpleGraph and get a\nsubgraph of the original graph.\n\n#source Subgraph.coeSubgraph --  G'.coe.Subgraph → G.Subgraph\n\n\nexample {A : G.Subgraph} (B : A.coe.Subgraph) (v w : V) : (A.coeSubgraph B).Adj v w\n    ↔ ∃ (hv : v ∈ A.verts) (hw : w ∈ A.verts), B.Adj ⟨_, hv⟩ ⟨_, hw⟩ :=\n  Subgraph.coeSubgraph_adj B v w\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.comap with Subgraph.hom gives Subgraph.restrict: given\nsubgraphs A and B of G, A.restrict B is the part of B that lies in A, as\na subgraph of A.coe (i.e. with vertex type A.verts).\n\n#source Subgraph.restrict\n\n\nVertices of A are adjacent in A.restrict B iff they are adjacent in both A and\nB.\n\nexample {A B : G.Subgraph} (v w : A.verts) :\n    (A.restrict B).Adj v w ↔ A.Adj v w ∧ B.Adj v w := Iff.rfl\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.4. Maps and Comaps","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps"},"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal":{"contents":"In this section we prove three classical sufficient conditions for a graph to be\nHamiltonian. The oldest two are Ore's theorem Ø. Ore, Note on Hamilton circuits, Amer. Math. Monthly 67 (1960), no. 1, 55. and its immediate\n(but earlier) corollary Dirac's theorem G. A. Dirac, Some theorems on abstract graphs, Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 2 (1952), no. 1, 69–81.. Call a finite graph an\nOre graph if the sum of the degrees of every pair of non-adjacent vertices is at\nleast the number of vertices in the graph. Ore's theorem says that every Ore graph on\nat least 3 vertices is Hamiltonian. The special case when the minimum degree is at\nleast half the number of vertices is Dirac's theorem.\n\nRather than prove Ore's theorem directly we prove the Bondy–Chvátal theorem\nJ. A. Bondy and V. Chvátal, A method in graph theory, Discrete Math. 15 (1976), no. 2, 111–135.: a graph is Hamiltonian iff its closure is, where the\nclosure is obtained by repeatedly joining non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is\nat least the number of vertices. Ore's theorem falls out immediately, since the\nclosure of an Ore graph is complete and complete graphs are Hamiltonian\n(isHamiltonian_top in Mathlib).\n\nRecall that if u ≠ v then G ⊔ edge u v is the graph obtained from G by adding\nthe edge u — v.\n\nThe proof has four steps.\n\n1. A maximal path (one that cannot be extended at either end) whose endpoints\nsatisfy Ore's degree condition can be closed up into a cycle visiting exactly the\nsame vertices. This uses Pósa rotations and a counting argument.2. If u and v are distinct non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is at least\n|V|, then G ⊔ edge u v is Hamiltonian iff G is. Adding an edge can only\nhelp, so the hard part is the converse: a Hamiltonian cycle of G ⊔ edge u v\ngives one of G. If the cycle avoids the new edge u — v, it already lies in\nG. Otherwise, deleting u — v leaves a Hamiltonian path from u to v in G.\nA Hamiltonian path is certainly maximal, so step 1 closes it up into a Hamiltonian\ncycle of G.3. Adding such edges repeatedly, in rounds, shows that a graph is Hamiltonian iff\nits closure is.4. Ore's and Dirac's theorems follow, since the closure of an Ore graph is complete.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.9. Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","id":"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal"}});